{"name":"Bavarian Hops","hex":"#acbf93","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bavarian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of custard-like food, made with fruit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bavarian"},{"term":"hops","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of hop (“jump”)","term of address for a guy."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of hop (“jump”)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hops"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hops","blocks":[{"text":"Hops are the green flowers of the hop plant Humulus lupulus, a member of the Cannabaceae family of flowering plants. They are used primarily as a bittering, flavouring, and stability agent in beer, to which, in addition to bitterness, they impart floral, fruity, or citrus flavours and aromas. Hops are also used for various purposes in other beverages and herbal medicine. The hops plants have separate female and male plants, and only female plants are used for commercial production. The hop plant is a vigorous climbing herbaceous perennial, usually trained to grow up strings in a field called a hopfield, hop garden, or hop yard when grown commercially. Many different varieties of hops are grown by farmers around the world, with different types used for particular styles of beer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Humulus_Lupulus_Hopfendolde-mit-hopfengarten.jpg/330px-Humulus_Lupulus_Hopfendolde-mit-hopfengarten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hops"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Hops_on_the_Yakima_Reservation.jpg/500px-Hops_on_the_Yakima_Reservation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Hops_on_the_Yakima_Reservation.jpg/1280px-Hops_on_the_Yakima_Reservation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Hops_on_the_Yakima_Reservation.jpg/500px-Hops_on_the_Yakima_Reservation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fully grown hops bines ready for harvest on the Yakama Indian Reservation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHops_on_the_Yakima_Reservation.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Decorative_Hops.jpg/330px-Decorative_Hops.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Decorative_Hops.jpg/330px-Decorative_Hops.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Humulus on a house","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADecorative_Hops.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Yakima-Valley-Hop-Yard.jpg/500px-Yakima-Valley-Hop-Yard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Yakima-Valley-Hop-Yard.jpg/1280px-Yakima-Valley-Hop-Yard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Yakima-Valley-Hop-Yard.jpg/500px-Yakima-Valley-Hop-Yard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early season hop growth in a hop yard in the Yakima River Valley of Washington with Mount Adams in the distance","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYakima-Valley-Hop-Yard.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Chmelnice.jpg/500px-Chmelnice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Chmelnice.jpg/1280px-Chmelnice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedi